---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Buying Gift Cards With Crypto in 2026: The Real Options - Mobazha"
  description="Paxful and NoOnes are gone. Here is where you can still buy or sell gift cards with bitcoin, what changed, and how to avoid getting burned."
>
  <Header />
  <main class="pt-32 pb-20">
    <article class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 lg:px-8">
      <h1 class="text-4xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-[var(--color-text-primary)] sm:text-5xl">
        Buying gift cards with crypto: what is actually left
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-6 text-lg text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        Two years ago this was a crowded market. Today it is not. Two of the biggest platforms are gone, and the ones that remain do very different things.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        This page separates them, because "buy gift cards with crypto" covers two completely different activities.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mt-12 text-2xl font-bold text-[var(--color-text-primary)]">What changed</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        <strong>Paxful</strong> shut down in 2025 after a US enforcement action. It had processed over $6 billion and served around 10 million users.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        <strong>NoOnes</strong>, which took in Paxful's users, wound down in August 2026 after EU sanctions cut off its partners. It had 2.5 million users.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        Both were peer-to-peer platforms. Both let people trade gift cards for crypto. Both are gone.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        What is left falls into two categories that people constantly confuse.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mt-12 text-2xl font-bold text-[var(--color-text-primary)]">Category one: buying new cards from a shop</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        You pay crypto, you get a code. No counterparty, no negotiation, no risk of a stolen card.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        <strong>Bitrefill</strong> is the largest. It has sold more than 16 million gift cards for crypto and covers most countries.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        <strong>Coinsbee</strong> lists over 5,000 brands across 185 countries and accepts 250+ coins.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        <strong>TheBitcoinCompany</strong> focuses on gift cards and prepaid Visa and Mastercard, with discounts up to 50% on some brands.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        If you want a card right now, at face value or close to it, use one of these. This is the safe path and there is no reason to complicate it.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mt-12 text-2xl font-bold text-[var(--color-text-primary)]">Category two: trading with another person</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        This is where the old platforms lived. You have a card, someone else has crypto, and you agree on a rate. Usually the card sells below face value, because the buyer is taking on risk.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        That market still exists, but it has moved. Some of it is on community forums, some on Telegram, some with local agents. The rules matter more than the platform.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        Community trading groups often ban resale and account trading outright, and they treat unsolicited messages as a scam signal. Read the rules before you post. Telegram groups have no rules at all, and that is the problem with them.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mt-12 text-2xl font-bold text-[var(--color-text-primary)]">Where Mobazha fits</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        Mobazha is not a gift card shop. It is a marketplace where people trade with each other, including gift cards, digital goods, and physical items.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        The difference from the old platforms is the money handling. Payments go into 2-of-3 multisig escrow, so no single party can move them. If the two sides disagree, an independent arbitrator decides. There is no KYC, and sellers keep their own store and customer list.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        If you are selling a card you already own, this is the structure you want. If you just want to buy an Amazon card at face value, use Bitrefill.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mt-12 text-2xl font-bold text-[var(--color-text-primary)]">A safety rule worth remembering</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        Normal discounted gift cards trade at 85% to 90% of face value. Below 70% is not a good deal, it is a warning. Cards at that price are usually bought with stolen payment methods, and when the card gets clawed back, you are the one holding a dead code.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        No platform can protect you from that if you ignore it.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mt-12 text-2xl font-bold text-[var(--color-text-primary)]">How to start</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        If you are buying: use a shop. If you are selling: open a store, list the card with its face value and your asking price, and let the escrow do the rest.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-8">
        <a class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-brand-light)] underline" href="https://app.mobazha.org/">Open a store on Mobazha</a>
      </p>
    </article>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Layout>
